* Keep all deployment environments unselected by default.
* If the user selects multiple environments, evaluate all selected environments independently and reflect their results in the component decision.

6. Veracode SAST – Use Only Exact-Match Branch Mitigation Scores

The current SAST score shown in Go/No-Go is incorrect. Change the calculation to use only the mitigated score for an exactly matched, valid branch/application mapping.

Inspect the existing Veracode mapping and result fields. Apply the following rules:

* Accept the result only when the existing mapping-confidence field indicates an exact match, or the mapping status explicitly equals MATCH, according to the actual schema.
* Use the mitigated score from the correct SAST result associated with that exact branch/application mapping.
* Display the mitigated score itself, such as 100, 99, or 98.
* Do not display blocker counts, flaw counts, unrelated scores, or SCA metrics in place of the mitigated score.
* Do not use a result from a different branch, an approximate application match, or an unrelated scan.
* If the mapping is not exact, display an explicit status such as INVALID MAPPING or UNVERIFIED, rather than presenting a misleading score.
* If no applicable SAST score exists, display UNKNOWN or DATA UNAVAILABLE, as appropriate.

SAST Go/No-Go rule

* A valid, exact-match mitigated score of 100 passes the SAST check.
* A valid, exact-match mitigated score below 100 is a release blocker and makes the component NO-GO when SAST is enabled.
* Missing scores, invalid mappings, and unavailable results must not be treated as passing.
* Do not infer a score of 100 from missing findings, scan status alone, or any other field.
* Verify the meaning, data type, and range of the existing mitigated-score field before implementing the comparison.

